=== Bounded 18-minute observation window (appended, not part of zsim.log itself) ===
Monitored directly via a background poll loop reading this log file and
/proc/1077927/stat every 30s, with a heartbeat every 120s. Baseline at
window start: line_count=100, utime=383578 jiffies.

heartbeat iter=4   elapsed=120s  utime=383578->392460  line_count=100
heartbeat iter=8   elapsed=240s  utime=392460->401328  line_count=100
heartbeat iter=12  elapsed=360s  utime=401328->410264  line_count=100
heartbeat iter=16  elapsed=480s  utime=410264->419173  line_count=100
heartbeat iter=20  elapsed=600s  utime=419173->428083  line_count=100
heartbeat iter=24  elapsed=720s  utime=428083->437003  line_count=100
heartbeat iter=28  elapsed=840s  utime=437003->445924  line_count=100
heartbeat iter=32  elapsed=960s  utime=445924->454836  line_count=100
[window end, 1080s] utime=464441  line_count=100  RSS grew 297MB -> 417MB

Summary: across the full 1080-second (18-minute) bounded window, the
process consumed ~809 seconds of genuine CPU time (confirmed via
/proc/<pid>/stat's utime field advancing steadily every single interval
-- not a hung/blocked process), yet produced ZERO new [progress]
checkpoints -- line_count stayed at exactly 100 throughout. Every prior
2,000,000-cycle span (0->2M->4M->6M->8M->10M) took roughly 400s of
wall-clock time each. This span (10M onward) consumed over 2.5x that
much wall-clock time (and most of it as genuine, steady CPU work, not
idle waiting) without completing even one more 2,000,000-cycle span.

RSS grew by ~120MB during this same window with zero cycle-count
progress visible -- consistent with sdram_model.sv's mem_store
associative array growing from a large number of newly-touched,
previously-unseen SDRAM addresses.

=== THE ACTUAL BLOCKING CONDITION, FOUND IMMEDIATELY AFTER THE WINDOW ===
Attempting to write this very evidence file failed with "Disk quota
exceeded" (EDQUOT, not ENOSPC -- the shared /home filesystem itself
still showed 69G free at the time). Direct investigation: this run's
own m5_doom_exec_tb.zwdb waveform file had grown to 19,583,426,560 bytes
(~19.58 GB) -- more than double the 8.57GB captured by the PREVIOUS
(pre-D_FindIWAD-fix) run, and still actively growing. waveform_mode="off"
is confirmed (again) not honored for this run either. This is almost
certainly both: (a) the direct cause of hitting a per-user disk quota
that the shared filesystem's own 69G-free headroom did not protect
against, and (b) a very plausible contributor to the dramatic wall-clock
slowdown itself (sustained large-file write I/O competing with
simulation work on this specific multi-tenant host).

Action taken: sent SIGTERM to the runaway process (PID 1077927, a
zenith --daemon my own session had spawned for this run -- confirmed
via `ps -o user` before acting). It terminated cleanly (zombie state
within 2 seconds). Deleted the now-closed, 19.58GB zwdb file, which
restored the ability to write files at all (verified with a test write
immediately after). This is treated as a genuine, confirmed resource-
exhaustion blocking condition -- not a judgment call based on wall-clock
patience alone -- consistent with the standing instruction that
resource exhaustion is explicit grounds for intervention.

Decision: this run produced no new architectural checkpoint beyond
cycle=10,000,000 and is not being pursued further. Proceeding to the
smaller, already-prepared WAD preload verification test next, with
--zwdb-start-off applied from the start this time to prevent a repeat
of this exact failure mode.
